divStyle.left = `calc(var(--total-scale-factor) * ${left.toFixed(2)}px)`;
divStyle.top  = `calc(var(--total-scale-factor) * ${top.toFixed(2)}px)`;
```

---

### Step 3 — Set `font-size`

```js
const scaleFactorStr = "calc(var(--total-scale-factor) *";
divStyle.fontSize = `${scaleFactorStr}${(TextLayer.#minFontSize * fontHeight).toFixed(2)}px)`;
//                 = `calc(var(--total-scale-factor) * <fontHeight>px)`
// where fontHeight = Math.hypot(tx[2], tx[3])   (the scale magnitude from the matrix)
// and TextLayer.#minFontSize is the browser's minimum rendered font size (usually 1.0)
```

---

### Step 4 — Apply CSS `transform` (in `#layout`)

```js
// #layout() is called after #appendText sets the base position and font-size

let transform = "";

// If browser enforces a minimum font size > 1px, scale it back down
if (TextLayer.#minFontSize > 1) {
  transform = `scale(${1 / TextLayer.#minFontSize})`;
}

// If the text has multiple characters, measure its actual rendered width
// then scaleX so it matches the PDF's specified width exactly
if (properties.canvasWidth !== 0 && properties.hasText) {
  const { width } = ctx.measureText(div.textContent);
  if (width > 0) {
    transform = `scaleX(${canvasWidth * this.#scale / width}) ${transform}`;
  }
}

// If the item is rotated, prepend rotate()
if (properties.angle !== 0) {
  transform = `rotate(${properties.angle}deg) ${transform}`;
}

if (transform.length > 0) {
  style.transform = transform;   // e.g. "rotate(90deg) scaleX(0.97) scale(1)"
}
```

---

### Summary — What each style property comes from

| CSS property | Source |
|---|---|
| `left` | `tx[4]` (translateX) ± ascent correction, as `%` of page width |
| `top` | `tx[5]` (translateY) − ascent, as `%` of page height |
| `font-size` | `Math.hypot(tx[2], tx[3])` (scale magnitude of the composed matrix) × `--total-scale-factor` CSS var |
| `font-family` | `style.fontFamily` from the PDF font style cache |
| `transform: scaleX(...)` | Ratio of PDF-specified width to actual browser-measured text width |
| `transform: rotate(...)` | `Math.atan2(tx[1], tx[0])` converted to degrees |
| `transform: scale(...)` | `1 / TextLayer.#minFontSize` (compensates for browser min-font enforcement) |

The key insight for your search highlight implementation: `tx[4]` and `tx[5]` are the raw `left`/`top` in PDF user units (before page-size normalization), and `Math.hypot(tx[2], tx[3])` gives the font height. Both are derived from composing the page flip matrix `[1, 0, 0, -1, -pageX, pageY + pageHeight]` with each item's `geom.transform` array.

